# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity (§5.1 row 1). These values are the WHOLE of what differs between
# this stack and a firm deployment. There is no local auth mode and no
# bypass: no SKIP_AUTH, no anonymous mode, no trusted header (S29).
#
# OIDC_DISCOVERY_API is the one entry here that a firm deployment does not
# set at all, and it is not an auth mode by another name: it changes WHERE
# the keys are fetched from, never WHICH issuer is trusted or how a token is
# checked. The check itself is one code path in both environments.
#
# Locally: Keycloak, seeded from infra/keycloak/lexprompt-realm.json.
# In a firm: Entra, and the same five variables (apps/api reads them as
# API_ISSUER, API_AUDIENCE, API_SUBJECT_CLAIM, API_GROUPS_CLAIM and
# API_REQUIRED_CLAIMS — docker-compose.yml maps the OIDC_* names below onto
# those) would read
#   OIDC_ISSUER_BROWSER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0
#   OIDC_SUBJECT_CLAIM=oid
#   OIDC_REQUIRED_CLAIMS=tid=<tenant>
# and OIDC_DISCOVERY_API would be left unset (see below).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ONE issuer string, used by the browser AND validated by the API. Keycloak
# stamps `iss` from the host a token was obtained through, so the API has to
# demand the address the browser actually used; an earlier version pointed the
# API at the container-network name instead and every locally issued token was
# refused with "sign in again", forever.
OIDC_ISSUER_BROWSER=http://localhost:8088/realms/lexprompt

# Where the API FETCHES that issuer's keys, which is a different fact from
# which issuer it trusts. `localhost:8088` is the host's published port and
# resolves to nothing from inside the container network, so discovery goes
# over the compose service name.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET in a firm deployment. Entra is publicly reachable, so the
# two addresses coincide and apps/api defaults this to the issuer — the seam
# exists because a local stack needs it, not because a tenant does.
OIDC_DISCOVERY_API=http://keycloak:8080/realms/lexprompt
OIDC_AUDIENCE=lexprompt-api
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=lexprompt-web
OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email lexprompt-api
OIDC_SUBJECT_CLAIM=sub
OIDC_GROUPS_CLAIM=groups
# Comma-separated claim=value pairs the API additionally requires of every
# token (apps/api/src/config.ts's parseRequiredClaims — a literal "{}" is
# NOT accepted here and would fail to parse; "empty" means an empty value,
# which is what Keycloak needs since it issues no tenant claim to check).
OIDC_REQUIRED_CLAIMS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which processing jurisdictions this deployment permits.
#
# THERE IS NO DEFAULT, AND THIS LINE IS DELIBERATELY COMMENTED OUT. The
# gateway refuses to start until you set it. That is not an obstacle to work
# around; it is the one value nobody but you can supply.
#
# Which jurisdictions are acceptable follows from the contracts and data
# provisions YOU hold with YOUR provider — SCCs, a DPA, negotiated retention
# and training terms, settled with legal input long before anyone edited this
# file. The API key is just the interface to a service whose guarantees live
# in that contract. LexPrompt enforces the policy you declare here; it has no
# view of its own about which jurisdictions are acceptable, and a default
# value would be exactly such a view, applied silently on your behalf.
#
# Valid values: UK, EU, US, other (comma-separated). These are processing
# BLOCS, not ISO country codes — a German deployment declares EU, and there
# is no DE. It is UK, never GB.
#   OpenAI direct, Anthropic direct and OpenRouter process in the US.
#   The `recorded` provider (offline fixtures) declares `other`.
#
# Uncomment and set to match your own provisions, for example:
# GATEWAY_ALLOWED_JURISDICTIONS=UK,EU
# GATEWAY_ALLOWED_JURISDICTIONS=UK,EU,US
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One key per provider you have configured in models.json. Leave the rest
# empty. None of these ever leaves the gateway container.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (Stage 2 Task 1). There is nothing to set here on purpose.
#
# docker-compose.yml wires API_DATABASE_URL and API_DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL
# directly to fixed dev-only passwords (infra/postgres/init.sql creates the
# two roles those DSNs name), rather than interpolating them from this file,
# because there is no browser-facing value here the way there is for OIDC_*
# — nobody signs in as a Postgres role. A firm deployment supplies its own
# DSNs as Container App secrets; see the README for the Azure step.
#
# API_DATABASE_POOL_MAX is optional and defaults to 10; it is not set here
# either, for the same reason API_MAX_BODY_BYTES is not: a sane default is a
# property of the software, not something a local developer should have to
# discover they need to set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
